WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.110
Welcome to the first ever
online Wiesner Gallery Tour.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.680
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliffe,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.680
and I'm the manager of
the student arts programs

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.270
here at MIT.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.260
I'm also joined by
Sarah Hirzel, who

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.160
is the coordinator of the
Wiesner Student Art Gallery

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.770
and Dalma Foldesi
and Jung In Seo,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.320
both graduates of the MIT School
of Architecture and Planning

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.340
master's degree, and
they were class of 2020.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.380
Congratulations.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.880
This evening, we're
pleased for them

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.240
to present their exhibition
called Misalignments.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.440
It's a multipart exploration
of materiality and fabrication,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.530
both by machine and by hand.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.780
Dalma and Jung
examined how robots

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.230
are affecting the texture
of our built environment

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.090
and demonstrate
through their work

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.810
some ways that they might
subvert those trends.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.530
After a brief introduction by
Sarah about the exhibition,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.140
Dalma and Jung will lead us
through their virtual show

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.400
via screen share.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.450
Without further ado, I'm going
to pass it over to Sarah.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.710
My name is Sarah Hirzel.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.110
I'm the coordinator of the
Wiesner Student Art Gallery,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.120
which is a space open
to any art practitioners

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.160
in any medium from any part
of MIT who are currently

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.900
enrolled us students.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.630
So keep that in mind if you're
interested in pursuing any kind

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.460
of project of this kind.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.950
Back on March 11, I
was really excited,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.290
because Dalma and Jung
were busy installing

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:26.880
a show in the Wiesner Gallery
in W20 in the student center.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.490
We were going to have an
opening the following week.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.990
They had plans for
an installation

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.460
with custom-made podiums.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.470
They were going to
shape the walls all

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.700
to support really exquisite work
that you're going to see here.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.820
The show was halfway built
when we were sent home

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.290
by the Institute.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.660
And though it's
really disappointing

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.420
that we can't share these really
subtle and beautiful pieces

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.490
with all of you in
person, I could not

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.290
be more thrilled with
the way that they

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.290
transformed that exhibition
into this opportunity.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.990
And I think that the site
that they made as a gallery

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.230
really illuminates their
research, their process,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.820
and their vision for all of us.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.330
As you'll see, these two
architect artists are--

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.120
they are unusually
well suited to bridge

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.110
the gap between the handmade
or the in-person experience

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.410
and the mechanized
world of technology.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.780
They were the perfect candidates
for this to solve this problem.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.350
So leave it to them to
take our expectations

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.678
and turn them upside down.

00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:29.220
I'm really grateful
that they're here

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.020
to help us re-envision
what the Wiesner can

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.050
be right now at this moment.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.850
So without further
ado, I just would

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.300
like to introduce Jung
In Seo and Dalma Foldesi.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.750
And I'd like to begin
by asking the two of you

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.880
to share a really basic
introduction to what you

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.850
do in layman's terms,
especially for people who've

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.740
never seen your work before.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.320
Thank you for the introductions,
Shannon Rose and Sarah.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.220
I'm Dalma.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.970
And I'm Jung.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.770
Thank you for joining us today.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.770
We're really excited
for this opportunity.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.220
And what we're
showing you today is

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.340
grounded in the work that began
as part of our master's thesis

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.450
at MIT.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.300
We reformatted it for
this virtual show,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.190
and we're very
much honored to be

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.330
part of the Arts on the Radar.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.240
And so the
thank-yous-- we really

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.300
want to thank everyone who
helped make this possible.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.600
So huge thank-you to Sarah,
Lydia, Andrea, and Heidi

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.590
from Arts at MIT and also to our
advisors, who are not here yet,

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.640
I don't think, Mariana,
Axel, and Mark.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.700
And then also to the
ceramics studios at MIT--

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:38.160
so Daryl, Jay, and thank you,
Graham, who's here also today.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.160
And also at Harvard,
Nathan, Kathy, Jeff, Kyle,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.980
and Casey, who
were super helpful.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.130
And last, the
fabrication team at MIT

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:51.580
Architecture-- so Chris, Jen,
Shah, Zane, Jay, and Jeff.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.810
Thank you so much for
making this possible.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:59.850
And I think with that, we
might attempt screen share

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.600
and see how that goes.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.300
First, a few words about the
design of the gallery itself

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.760
where you are
interested in exploring

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.760
what this virtual
format can afford us

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.410
given the scale and
materiality of our work

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:16.529
and the time-based media
that comes along with it.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.970
So we decided to go against
this one-dimensional experience

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:24.020
based on scrolling and image
and image grid layouts.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.330
But we also didn't want to
go in the other direction

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:31.020
where we create a game-like
3D environment in the browser.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:31.520
Yeah.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.570
So instead, what follows is
a series of rooms, for those

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.300
of you who haven't been there.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.860
It's sort of
skeuomorphic and familiar

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.680
in the way it's constructed
and its first impression.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.290
But later, it becomes
defamiliarized

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.530
as you begin to explore
the spaces a bit more.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.220
And we focused on this
non-linear exploration

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.530
of the galleries and
layering information

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.370
in the design of the UI,
the user interaction.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.160
And much like in
physical galleries,

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.820
at first you're immersed
in this full screen image.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.280
And later, you can
sort of uncover

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.700
details and nested
layers of information.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:15.770
And I think with that, we
can walk into the first room.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.590
Yeah, so our title,
Misalignment,

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.180
is discussed in this first room.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:27.400
And our interest is in the
conditions of misalignments

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.235
that are usually emerging
around the imprecise material

00:05:30.235 --> 00:05:32.140
assemblies and engineering.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.790
The title brings
us to the question

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.180
of exactitude and
ideal geometries, which

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.370
we specifically
wanted to interrogate

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.610
within the discipline
of architecture.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.310
And to delve into that
a little bit more,

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:50.500
we wanted to use the example
of these standard sized bricks.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.650
The example of bricks and the
history of the mechanization

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.390
of their production introduces
some of the key concepts

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.010
that this exhibition
works to destabilize.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:03.130
So in the development
of brick-making machines

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.820
in the US, which happened
throughout the 19th century,

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:10.450
this material, clay,
was controlled and made

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:13.390
to conform to these
ideal orthogonal,

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:18.040
preconceived geometries
of a certain size.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:18.580
Yeah.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:20.980
Ironically though,
these actions that

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.850
produced these precise
geometries were quite simple.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.160
They had to rely
on this simplicity.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.500
In this case, pressing
clay into a wooden box

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.370
to reproduce the standardized
dimensions within tolerance.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.380
These low fidelity
actions, as we're

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.440
going to start calling
them, almost mechanical,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.230
produce high
dimensional stability

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:44.410
by which we mean that the
final brick conformed to the 2

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:45.670
by 4 by 6--

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.110
that kind of
dimensions that they

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.950
required in standardized parts.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.510
So here, the quote that
you see kind of also

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:55.750
explains that as
men were molders

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.140
who would scoop up enough clay
for about a brick and a half,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.260
press it firmly into a
sand-sprinkled wooden box,

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.150
divide into
brick-sized sections,

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:06.190
and then scrape off
any excess material.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:09.370
So it's a very low fidelity,
simple action that you would

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:13.950
have to perform repetitively.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.540
Can you all see the screen OK?

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:17.280
Works fine.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:18.870
Cool.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:19.410
So--

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.240
Mm-hmm.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.520
Yeah, so-- oh, sorry.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.910
You were going to go.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.160
I mean, the more or
less exact diagram

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.600
that we show here expands
this notion of exactness based

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.370
on the two criteria that
we mentioned before,

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:36.640
one that is based on
the analysis of fidelity

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.780
of actions versus the
dimensional stability.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.760
The first axis,
the vertical axis,

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.760
locates the exactness in
the dimensional stability.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.780
How closely can you realize
some preconceived ideal geometry

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.220
within tolerance?

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.850
Typical of Western architecture
practices, these buildings that

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:58.420
follow this model are
designed to approximate some

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.650
very ideal geometries
drawn in plan and sections

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.950
through cycles of building
and then also correction,

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:10.340
decay, maintenance,
and restoration.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.340
Yes, so this is the first
sort of vertical axis

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:15.050
that you see here.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.990
And on the other
hand, this horizontal

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:22.670
axis that we're introducing
sort of understands exactness

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:25.430
in terms of the fidelity
of gestures as opposed

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.080
to dimensions.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.240
This is the fidelity of actions.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.960
And for example, in vernacular
mud and clay construction,

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.159
the objective is not to
achieve some preconceived ideal

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:40.070
geometry and
dimensions, but rather

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:46.070
to find a form through layers
of carefully performed actions.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:49.270
Even though these actions may
not seem dimensionally precise

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:52.660
per se, these builders have
a very fine-tuned sensibility

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.000
to the material that
they are working with.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.600
And their actions are
precise in that term.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.570
These precise actions
allow the thin structures

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.610
to stand and not collapse.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.920
And in doing so, they
better adapt to the material

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.670
availability and accommodate
material qualities,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.770
sometimes letting the
material qualities to emerge

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:14.700
rather than suppressing it.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:21.090
And so throughout this
show, we're kind of also

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:25.020
working within this diagram
but different from what

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.740
contemporary digital
fabrication projects tend to do,

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.650
which tend to still
inhabit the first axis.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.130
And designers evaluate
tools and designs

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:38.850
based almost solely on
dimensional stability.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.970
And they still aim to find
ever final layers, for example,

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:47.400
in 3D-printed buildings, almost
dematerializing those buildings

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.990
into pure geometries.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.870
And this is often at
the expense of resources

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.120
or overlooking
material qualities

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.970
that we hope to recover.

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:56.470
Yeah.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.620
And instead what
we want to explore

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:03.300
is how do we expand the spectrum
afforded by this diagram?

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:04.350
What does it mean?

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.830
Like, what if, instead of
optimizing dimensions or even

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:11.700
actions, we wandered
into this space?

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.910
More and more-- and sometimes
you start and more and more,

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:17.760
and you go into this
other zone of the diagram.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.250
What does it mean to wander
in this space as opposed

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.800
to always trying to optimize,
always going into the finer

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.610
and finer precision?

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.860
Can we think of
design as trajectories

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.560
rather than goal product
fixed in time and space?

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.260
So these are the questions that
we are revisiting in every room

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.280
essentially in different scales.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.570
And so in really basic
terms, our project

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:46.600
deals with these
questions of tolerance.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.950
And we were using this
industrial robotic arm

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.670
and our own hand gestures.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.130
And we were trying to expand
this definition of exactness.

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:57.880
This will come up.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:01.060
It's something that's a more--

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:04.055
that would admit more error
in error-prone processes.

00:11:04.055 --> 00:11:05.270
Mm-hmm.

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:09.500
And we fabricated all the
objects in the exhibition

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:11.290
either by hand or
with this KUKA KR

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.630
6 R900 industrial
robotic arm that

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:18.530
sits at the MIT
architecture department.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:22.460
But these arms have proliferated
in various design departments

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:25.610
across the country,
diverting them

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.280
from their original
roles in manufacturing.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.060
And while they were designed
to perform precise and complex

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.900
actions in a loop, designers
now push these robots

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:39.920
to go beyond this original aim
of optimizing and accelerating

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.810
manufacturing.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.000
Yet still, many
of these practices

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.960
still follow the first axis
of dimensional stability--

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.020
so getting something to fit
that prescribed dimension

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.040
as a model.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.870
And they often obscure
the interventions

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:58.760
of the hand to create this
illusion of total automation.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:11:59.260
Yeah.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:03.820
So we want to kind of propose
a different approach that

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.820
celebrates this interaction
between mechanical actions

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.230
and hand gestures.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:12.260
Sorry.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.410
We developed machines and tools
to collaborate with these arms

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:18.680
rather than trying to
always depend on the arm.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.830
We adopted traditional trimming
and carving tools as well as

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:25.400
creating custom nozzles
and custom machines,

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:28.040
even though they were kind
of rudimentary in the actions

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.660
that it performs.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:34.280
These low tolerance machines
accommodates high tolerance

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:35.960
gestures and forms.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.720
We learned from existing
open source extruder designs

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.960
and from the craft of
traditional ceramics together.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:45.290
And we decided to design our own
tools to expand and understand

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:46.720
our design space.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:47.220
Mm-hmm.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:51.190
So now we're going to be
entering the next room.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:55.310
So we'll get to see
these machines at work.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:13:00.370
So this next room we
call the room of actions.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:02.890
This room gives you
a glimpse or maybe

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.140
a preview of some
of the emerging

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:09.910
complex formal language of
these actions that we explored.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:13.050
And they kind of work with the--

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:14.740
I mean, traditional
pottery techniques

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:19.600
of coiling and carving
or even stacking clay.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.360
And so, for example,
these white coils

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:28.330
here illustrate this behavior
of rope coiling, which

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.940
is a behavior that other
non-Newtonian fluids also

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:34.150
perform when extruded--
so even glass or honey.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:38.410
And like honey dribbling,
these extruded coils

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:41.680
fold, swirl, and
then slowly solidify

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:47.080
into a seemingly precarious
form deviating heavily

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.270
from the ideal,
preconceived form,

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:53.110
but still finding
stability in the end.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:57.830
So also I think we
have some images

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:02.690
of the process and the
nozzles that we designed.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:07.790
And we'll get back to seeing
this in action in a bit.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:12.780
And one other example we wanted
to bring up is the carving.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.400
The carving is kind
of a risky balancing

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.310
act between material behavior
and prescribed geometry,

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:23.410
because the carving action
removes the excess material

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.020
created by coiling.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:29.020
And it, in a way, gets closer
to the original prescribed form.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.820
Or sometimes, depending on the
tolerances of the coiled form,

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:36.760
it can make a very dramatic
puncture such as this opening.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:40.750
So this was the
tool that we used

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:43.210
to carve this form
where we began

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.490
with this layer
of coiling actions

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.740
that varies on the tolerances.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:51.340
And as a result, when you
carve away from this form,

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.450
at some locations you get
this very uniform surface

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:58.870
qualities, where in
these other cases where,

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:02.260
because of the low tolerances
of the surface that

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.060
was developed before, this
creates a very dramatic

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:05.560
opening.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:06.380
Mm-hmm.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.305
So it's about calibrating
those openings,

00:15:08.305 --> 00:15:12.220
and even though not determining
their size precisely.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.100
And in order to get--

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:20.130
and we'll let you explore
some of the other things

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:21.720
in this room later on.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.980
But to get here to these
sort of complex textures

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:31.830
and layered actions, we first
mapped out the expanded space

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:35.160
off the diagram to create
a series of objects

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.260
that we called
experiments, which you see

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:40.680
in this room on this platform.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:44.310
So on this platform
here, the experiments

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:47.940
populate the diagram that
you saw in the first room.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:52.500
And by fixing the ideal geometry
with these torqued ellipses,

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.810
we were able to map out
the different qualities

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:59.400
and textures and forms and
behaviors at the edges--

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.550
at all of the edges and in
the middle of this diagram.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:08.010
And you see the lower left, from
collapsing into the formless

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:12.840
in the upper right corner to
minimal, almost imperceptible

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:17.240
layers that we would normally
define as exact under I

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.760
guess the axis of
dimensional stability.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.400
We kind of explored
this full space.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.760
And throughout that
creation, these objects

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:29.750
are not really fixed
in a particular form.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:33.080
They can travel in this diagram
as long as they're kept moist

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.330
and they are not fired.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:37.610
And sometimes they
can suddenly collapse,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.175
or you can even
carve away from them.

00:16:40.175 --> 00:16:44.060
So as they go further away from
a certain degree of exactness,

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.920
they can also come back to it.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.210
So tolerance is also
change over time.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:56.800
So these are just some examples.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:57.300
Yeah.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.220
Also in this gallery, we
wanted to reveal something

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:04.270
about the process of
creating this exhibition.

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.869
In order to bring our
objects into virtual spaces,

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:10.869
we actually had to create
digital copies of all of them.

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:12.970
Exposing this
digital materiality

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:15.819
was something that
we thought it was

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:21.130
interesting to expose the
underside of it, so to speak.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.440
So this is essentially
what's called a texture map.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:28.000
So this is a square
image that locates

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:32.780
pixels of the scanned
mesh that you see earlier

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:39.290
to give it some imitation
or appearance of, like,

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:42.080
giving it a texture
that's lifelike texture.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:45.980
And here we were
interested in this idea

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:48.770
of digital materiality
showing through

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:52.260
and how that relates to this
digital virtual exhibition

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.590
and how it gives
it the materiality.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:00.570
And it's sort of one
attempt at flattening

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:03.870
these three-dimensional
objects here

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:07.010
and also gives you a
peek at the construction

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:08.000
of the gallery itself.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:08.840
Mm-hmm.

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:13.120
And from this room
of experiments,

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:15.110
we go over to the
room of studies,

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:19.460
which builds on the experiments,
similar to the details

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:22.070
that you saw earlier.

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.160
This moves beyond
those single actions

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:29.640
and imbues those actions
with intention and layers

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.130
them to create more
complex expressions.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:38.990
So for example, this vault
here uses precise angles

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:43.400
to build an enclosure without
any additional support.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.610
And first it's coiled with
a nozzle at specific angles

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:49.010
to construct the
walls, and then carving

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:52.880
can create openings and remove
some of the excess material.

00:18:56.528 --> 00:18:57.950
Yeah.

00:18:57.950 --> 00:19:01.580
And in this example, we
wanted to exploit the idea

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:05.060
of gradients and stability.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.100
So where we began
on the bottom, we

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:11.510
add very fine layers,
almost imperceptible.

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:14.360
And we increased-- we
modulated the layer height

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:16.820
so that we get this
change in the material

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:20.720
behavior in the middle
and then retracting back

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.640
to this original condition.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:27.140
And this is kind of going
beyond our initial studies

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:30.360
where we were fixing
this kind of parameters.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:32.090
Now we're playing
with these parameters

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:37.590
to see what kind of
qualities we can get out of.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:45.240
Yeah, and maybe last, this
other object kind of improvised.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:48.810
We were interested in
hybridizing more explicitly

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.090
both hand and machine action.

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:53.850
So the first layer, the
bottom of this vault

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.620
was hand constructed
and 3D scanned.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:58.290
So it sort of went
back and forth

00:19:58.290 --> 00:20:03.600
between the digital and the
actual physical construction.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.170
And based on those
first couple layers,

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:12.630
we then found a form that
would create this enclosure

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:15.030
and slowly built it.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.180
And we invite you to explore
all these other objects

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:18.799
in this room.

00:20:18.799 --> 00:20:19.299
Mm-hmm.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:27.240
Here, if the earlier room where
we discussed about the texture

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.330
maps was one kind of
flattening the scan meshes,

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.510
this room presents
another way to capture

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:35.670
in two dimensions the
tool path and the actions

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.560
by the bare hands.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.100
These two are
radically different,

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:43.710
like one that's seemingly
very organized and regular,

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:50.130
and there's this other layer
where it seems very chaotic.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:56.580
These are collaborative in a way
that they reveal the hands that

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:59.220
assists and adjusts
designs with and sometimes

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:01.810
altering the planned path.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.620
Even if they're
different, these two paths

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.440
are very much coordinated.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.660
They are traces in a
different sense than what

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:09.580
we had discussed earlier.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:11.500
They don't draw the
final object per se,

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:15.820
but they show the dialogue of
the mechanical and mathematical

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.746
movements of the tool and
organic gestures of the hand.

00:21:20.746 --> 00:21:24.800
Yeah, and while
this room, I think,

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.290
captures these
actions frozen in time

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:31.910
and flattened out on these
surfaces, the next room

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:36.470
and the final room that
we'll show you today

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:41.090
shows the machines and the
hand in action in time.

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:42.800
So this is a little video room.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:49.640
And this might be sort of slow
and jagged over our really slow

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:50.600
internet connection.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.460
But we'll try to play it.

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:55.670
And I think I didn't
hit Optimize for Video.

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:59.630
So I could also just
restart the screen share.

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:04.880
But this might be
a good point for us

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:07.820
to stop and let the video
speak for themselves

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.700
and open up for Q&amp;A.

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:11.120
Yeah.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:12.800
Thank you very much.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:17.270
Thank you all so much
for coming out here.

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:19.040
I am just curious
your conception

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:23.180
about how this research is
scalable to architecture--

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:26.570
if you see this work as ending
in the theoretical space

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:29.480
or an art object that
contains an idea,

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:33.260
or if you think that
a robotic fabrication

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:38.880
of this type of material could
be done at a larger scale.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:48.310
I think there are attempts
now that are being--

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:49.230
in the media.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:54.410
For example, like, the Mars
project that they put it into,

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:55.600
like, discussions before.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:56.920
Mm-hmm.

00:22:56.920 --> 00:23:01.955
Where there are attempts
to go at a bigger scale

00:23:01.955 --> 00:23:04.240
of this technology itself.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:07.510
But what I think we are
missing at the point is,

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:10.630
again, this expanded
understanding

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:13.600
of why we need to be
always so precise.

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:19.900
And can we go beyond this narrow
perception of the exactness?

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:26.810
And I think in that sense, that
question exists in any scale

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:28.760
that we were working with.

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:33.660
But we would love to try
this at a larger scale.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.980
In fact, we are
trying to discuss it.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:41.210
Well, I think-- yeah, I think we
discussed this a lot of times,

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:44.180
both with the different build
spaces that we've been in

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:49.520
and especially for this virtual
exhibition where we could have

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:54.110
easily made a larger inhabitable
structure of the same things

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:59.120
that we've been working on
just by using the scans.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:00.860
I mean, scale is
definitely something

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:04.880
that we could play with
in a virtual setting.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:07.700
But we sort of naturally
moved away from that,

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:09.080
because I think--

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.650
yeah, I think while we would
be interested in maybe scaling

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:16.910
them, but it would be a very
different outcome from what you

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:19.280
see in this particular show.

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:24.450
I think we would definitely
have to rethink a lot of things,

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:27.560
not just forms, but even
what we're contributing

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:30.560
to that discussion beyond.

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:34.130
And I think these
objects even might

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:39.140
be scalable as wall sections
or something like that.

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:42.710
I think what we're trying
to think more about next

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:46.670
is more about interaction
with the machine

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:51.200
rather than making a
building out of this.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:53.870
Yeah, because each
project that we-- like,

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:57.350
that two different
trajectories of our project

00:24:57.350 --> 00:25:00.380
takes a different amount
of time in terms of,

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:02.000
like, what we can achieve.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.430
Like, if we were to
go big right now,

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:07.220
then we would have to
dedicate a lot of time just

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:10.430
to scale things up
without really exploring

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.530
further questions about it.

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:14.840
So right now I
think, as Dalma said,

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:20.540
our next interest is in
the interactive part.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.040
Tiandra has a question.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.030
What did you find
most compelling

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:29.680
about the virtual
exhibition that

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:32.440
may not have manifested in
the original physical version?

00:25:36.499 --> 00:25:38.170
I think-- hmm.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:38.670
Hi, Tiandra.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.040
[LAUGHS]

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:42.810
Thank you for the questions.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:46.440
Yeah, I think one thing--

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:48.420
I guess one thing I
mentioned that we didn't do

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:54.360
is scale up and use that idea
of scaling for these objects.

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:59.850
One thing that we played
with was that non-linear way

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:01.860
of exploring and
preventing people

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:05.590
from going backwards, which
sort of gives these limitations,

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:10.740
but also, like, unique
ways of exploring

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.900
these different galleries.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:17.080
And I think just setting up
a very conventional gallery

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.960
setting, which is not at
all what these objects--

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:23.246
like, something that these
objects speak against

00:26:23.246 --> 00:26:26.720
is also an interesting
part of it.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:27.760
Yeah.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:32.990
I mean, one of our meta goal
was to think about, yes,

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:36.220
like, if you're doing this
in virtual exhibition,

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:39.250
what can we get out
of this, playing

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:41.320
with this virtual
exhibition as a format?

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:45.700
And you might have noticed
that the gravity doesn't

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:47.530
hold true in this
virtual exhibition

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:51.880
anymore as you walk through
a couple of different rooms.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:55.030
So that was, like,
one cute foray

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.770
into what are the bigger
questions that we can ask.

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:01.570
And like, how do we
represent drawings?

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:03.340
How do we represent models?

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:07.390
And, like, the 3D scan became
a big protagonist in this

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:09.760
where it became--

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:12.610
it gave us this idea of,
like, digital materiality

00:27:12.610 --> 00:27:16.480
where you have to jump through
a lot of loops of documenting it

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:22.360
in 3D scans that
we didn't think of.

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:25.350
And that's kind of already a
part of our process anyways.

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:25.890
Yeah.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:27.598
Because we're always
going back and forth

00:27:27.598 --> 00:27:30.330
between the physical and
the virtual even though

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:33.150
it seems more physical.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.560
Like, when we
carve an object, we

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:37.080
would need to have a
scan of it in order

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:40.440
to be able to
carve it or perform

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:43.620
any other actions on them.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:45.210
So that was interesting.

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:47.800
Mm-hmm.

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.170
We have another question.

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:49.770
Oh, yeah.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.585
It looks like Graham
has his hand raised.

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:56.380
I do.

00:27:56.380 --> 00:28:00.550
I really enjoyed
how you found value

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:03.200
in all the different
output in this project.

00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:05.590
So whether it was
the experiments

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:14.970
getting their own room or
translating the 3D scan to a 2D

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:22.700
image or the tool paths, your
ability to find importance

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:26.570
or to see what each of
these steps had to offer

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:28.050
was really inspiring.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:35.200
The process of your process
is just exciting to me.

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:39.120
And so I want to
commend you on that.

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:46.590
I would love to hear you talk
about the seam in each of the--

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:48.800
not so much in this
shot, but the video that

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:53.010
was playing prior to this, the
tool pathing will go to a point

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:56.820
and then change direction and go
back and then change direction.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:58.670
So it goes
counterclockwise, clockwise.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:04.560
And in each of the experiments,
there's a distinct seam.

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:05.100
Cue it up.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:06.740
Look at that.

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:11.880
Could you just talk about what
its role in the project was?

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:16.780
And I will mute and
listen to your response.

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:17.280
Yeah.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:19.290
I think it was a
big question for us

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:23.910
that we never really ever
talked about, I don't think.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:28.930
But normally people try
to not have a seam at all.

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:33.330
They would just go in circles
and, like, slightly step up

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:36.850
maybe just like a really
small gradient all the way.

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:42.300
So it just seems like one
continuous coiled thing.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:44.910
And I guess if you're minimizing
the layers, then it's, like,

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:46.080
completely--

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.630
it's not noticeable at all.

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:52.680
And we wanted to play with
openings though, and especially

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.395
with something
that would support

00:29:54.395 --> 00:29:55.770
the weight of a
clay on top of it

00:29:55.770 --> 00:30:00.210
like in this case where it's
becoming this crazy opening

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:05.170
here, not necessarily to make
a door or anything like that,

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:08.730
but just to see if we
could use this really

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.950
unstable way of making--

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:14.060
like closing up that seam.

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:16.590
And the seam actually kind
of goes around the object.

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:19.150
So it kind of
defines its own path.

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:19.650
Yeah.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:21.870
And also in the--

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:22.665
I wanted to show--

00:30:22.665 --> 00:30:24.740
I think we need to let--

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:25.770
a bit slow now.

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:26.340
Yeah.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:30.480
In the example in the
experiments, the seam

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:33.030
itself helps to give
it a directionality

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:33.890
between each layer.

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:36.620
So some of them the beaded--

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:38.490
we call beaded
one is essentially

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:44.670
done by pausing after every
few seconds or something.

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:47.640
And so because of
that, if you go

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:51.090
in one direction for
one there and then

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:55.260
the other direction
for other layer, then

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.994
you get this alternating effect.

00:30:57.994 --> 00:31:01.540
It's maybe not so
visible in this one.

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:02.400
Mm-hmm.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.770
But for example, there.

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:07.050
Was this done on
the same direction?

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:09.660
So, like, there were--

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:13.200
by introducing this,
like, unstable condition,

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.230
we actually opened up a lot
of different possibilities

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:19.773
rather than just giving
you a vase-like form.

00:31:19.773 --> 00:31:21.310
Can't go back now.

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:22.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.230
Can't go back.

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:26.830
So that was one
of our intentions

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:28.570
in introducing the seam.

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:32.920
And plus, like, it gives
us a challenge of how

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:35.560
do we make this
structural, like,

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:39.510
if you can't rely on
this being a closed form.

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:42.860
I think you can see it inside
here, but not the best view.

00:31:46.596 --> 00:31:47.530
[LAUGHS]

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:49.700
Close.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:55.190
For me, it acts as a
invitation since the form

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:59.390
isn't closed or closed in
a traditional sort of sense

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:02.780
where you would go
in through the top.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:04.700
It acts-- like, as
a viewer, for me, it

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:08.160
acts as an invitation to
stay and look at that object.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:11.930
And so by breaking
my expectations

00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:16.400
of the ceramic objects, it just
holds my attention a little bit

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:17.580
longer.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:19.640
I enjoy that choice.

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:24.410
And then it sort of trails
through and leaves its mark

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:25.500
around the whole project.

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:28.620
So thank you for that.

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:29.120
Thank you.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:32.910
I see that Maddie
has her hand raised.

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:33.580
Awesome.

00:32:33.580 --> 00:32:35.580
I think we have also
questions in the chat.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:36.080
Hi.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:37.040
Hi.

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:39.932
I'm wondering if you can
talk a little bit about kind

00:32:39.932 --> 00:32:41.390
of the process of
how you developed

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:42.500
the different materials
that you used.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.390
It looks like there's a lot
of different types of clay

00:32:44.390 --> 00:32:45.860
and different
materials you used.

00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:47.960
Can you talk about,
like, where you

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:49.790
started in the
process of deciding

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:52.492
which of those were helpful--

00:32:52.492 --> 00:32:53.700
Started with Graham, I think.

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:56.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:59.850
Graham's clay donations to us.

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:02.930
At first, we had very little
understanding of clay,

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:04.980
the material itself.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:08.000
And so before even
starting this project,

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:13.880
we just ended up signing up
for the ceramics class up at SA

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:17.230
and actually learning a
little bit about how to--

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:21.560
like more traditional pottery
techniques and hand building

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:23.810
and wheel throwing.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:28.070
And we realized that there's
this insanely broad and diverse

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:31.205
world of different
clay materials.

00:33:31.205 --> 00:33:36.500
It's not like there's one kind
of clay or ceramic material.

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:39.810
It's just a huge range
of things that each

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:41.660
have their specific qualities.

00:33:41.660 --> 00:33:44.510
And a lot of these objects
depend on those qualities

00:33:44.510 --> 00:33:46.940
to become what they are.

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:50.490
And not only the
actual raw material,

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:52.760
but also how moist
it is and how it's

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:58.110
mixed to compose these objects
was a huge part of this study.

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:02.480
And I think that's also why you
see these objects especially

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:05.300
kind of showcased all the
different materials that we

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:07.310
tested.

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:09.739
And I think it's still
an open exploration,

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:14.340
because they definitely lead
to such different outcomes.

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:14.840
Yeah.

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:20.150
And also-- yeah, we wanted to
reiterate that the complexity

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:24.949
is in the material more than
the machines themselves,

00:34:24.949 --> 00:34:27.389
because there are a lot of--

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.500
when we were researching some
other type of clay extruder

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:34.429
designs, the consensus
is that the difficulty

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:37.460
is in getting the clay
consistent, meaning,

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:40.090
like, it wasn't the problem
with the machines themselves.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:43.909
It was a problem with the way
that we handled the material.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:45.949
And the material is, like, so--

00:34:45.949 --> 00:34:47.449
it can be so broad.

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:50.420
Clay, if you start
dissecting it,

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:53.949
is a combination of a
lot of different things.

00:34:53.949 --> 00:34:54.800
Yeah.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:58.130
And so when we took
the class at Harvard,

00:34:58.130 --> 00:35:01.510
we were also very curious, can
we mix glass pieces into it?

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:06.540
Can we use the fired pieces
broken down into smaller pieces

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:09.350
and what they called
grog, I guess?

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:13.460
Can we incorporate
that into giving us

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:17.040
this, like, very smooth texture
versus a very rough texture?

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:22.460
Like, for example,
these are very smooth,

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:24.350
because the grains are fine.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:26.990
And this one, for example, we
had, like, paper clay in it--

00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:28.310
like, paper clay--

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:29.590
paper fibers in it.

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:31.910
So when it's extruding
from the same extruder,

00:35:31.910 --> 00:35:34.350
you get this very
different qualities.

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:37.810
And so those are
still open questions.

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:38.315
Mm-hmm.

00:35:38.315 --> 00:35:39.940
And I think the design
of that material

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:42.190
is, like, a critical thing
that's at the heart of this.

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:42.690
Totally.

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:45.490
Great.

00:35:45.490 --> 00:35:45.970
Thank you.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:46.470
Mm-hmm.

00:35:46.470 --> 00:35:47.600
Mm-hmm.

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:50.090
I see that we also have
a question from Carla.

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:53.090
Do you think it goes against
the machine's essence

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:54.950
in not being exact?

00:35:54.950 --> 00:36:00.140
And how could that redefine
how we use machinery?

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:04.670
I think it's not going
against machines as a sense

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:05.780
in not being exact.

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:10.310
Machines have always been
inexact in the tolerance.

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:14.660
So the tolerance has been
getting smaller every iteration

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:16.250
of the machine designs.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:19.730
But essentially,
how much error can

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:21.560
you tolerate is the
question rather than

00:36:21.560 --> 00:36:22.820
the machine being exact.

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:24.770
Machines have--
there was this, like,

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:27.370
really interesting
reading from--

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.870
I forgot the guy's
name, but Ken Graham?

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:32.600
No.

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:34.700
I forgot the
author's name, but he

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:38.420
was discussing how the
most complex aircraft

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:40.280
parts were actually made--

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:44.210
hammered by these craftsmen
when the machines were

00:36:44.210 --> 00:36:49.490
failing to produce, like,
the super precise geometry.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:51.960
The craftsmen were
able to see it.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:54.590
And actually, through
looking at their reflections,

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:57.500
they were able to discern it way
better than the machines could

00:36:57.500 --> 00:36:58.560
do.

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:03.380
So this is of like,
we're trying to say

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:06.530
machines are never that exact.

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:09.240
Like, it's a conception
that we have.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.550
It's a conception that the
building should be exact

00:37:11.550 --> 00:37:14.830
that we want to dismantle.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:19.480
And I don't think it's
going against a sense.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:23.230
We're trying to
blend the essence.

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:26.733
Back to Tiandra's
other question about

00:37:26.733 --> 00:37:28.150
if there's something
to be learned

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:30.520
by a sculptor or potter.

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:32.590
I guess we kind of
semi answered that

00:37:32.590 --> 00:37:35.600
by saying that that's
where we started.

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:38.350
And we're not very
good at it yet.

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:41.770
But we're hoping to get
back to it once, I guess,

00:37:41.770 --> 00:37:44.915
ceramics facilities reopen.

00:37:44.915 --> 00:37:47.770
But I think there's
definitely a lot of exchange

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:49.070
to be made there.

00:37:49.070 --> 00:37:53.110
And we actually, as part
of the Harvard classes,

00:37:53.110 --> 00:37:55.840
I think-- and also maybe that
could be a really cool thing

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:58.000
to have at MIT as well.

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:01.740
But the ceramics artists
have been working with robots

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:04.900
and working with
architecture students like us

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:07.390
and sort of building that
exchange between the two

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:09.580
disciplines and two very
different ways of thinking

00:38:09.580 --> 00:38:11.770
or making.

00:38:11.770 --> 00:38:14.240
And I think bringing
those together--

00:38:14.240 --> 00:38:17.520
there's a really cool
opportunity for that.

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:18.040
Yeah.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:18.540
Totally.

00:38:18.540 --> 00:38:21.270
I mean, we can't
emphasize how much

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:26.250
we learned before we even began
this from the clay classes

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:28.230
that we took at MIT.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:29.580
Mm-hmm.

00:38:29.580 --> 00:38:31.870
Shout out to Stacy.

00:38:31.870 --> 00:38:32.370
Exactly.

00:38:36.770 --> 00:38:38.240
Ooh, all right.

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:39.110
Here we go.

00:38:39.110 --> 00:38:41.540
Andrea, our esteemed
CAMIT director,

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:43.850
says that she is a great
admirer of this project

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:46.400
because of how it
complicates the relationship

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.580
between the hand and the
machine and also because it's

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:51.290
one of the first
projects that she's

00:38:51.290 --> 00:38:53.000
been able to follow
to fruition that

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:55.580
was funded by a CAMIT grant.

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:57.200
Could you reflect
on how that grant

00:38:57.200 --> 00:39:01.690
helped you extend beyond where
your thesis work stopped?

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:03.980
And also, everybody,
there's a CAMIT plug.

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:07.810
So please do check out
the link in the chat.

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:12.190
I think we had
lots of plans for--

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:14.860
and, like, great
opportunities also

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:18.520
for doing something
very different from what

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:21.070
we had in mind initially.

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:22.480
And so as we were--

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:28.480
we were actually about to go
to another facility in March

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:29.455
when--

00:39:29.455 --> 00:39:34.890
and we did, I think, one shop
training when MIT closed,

00:39:34.890 --> 00:39:37.390
and everything else closed.

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:39.450
And that facility
still hasn't reopened.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:47.140
So I think we ended up just--

00:39:47.140 --> 00:39:50.120
the little time that
we had before that,

00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:51.760
we worked on similar--

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:54.520
I think all of the larger
things that we made

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:57.860
and the things in
the studies room

00:39:57.860 --> 00:40:01.090
were part of the
exploration that we began.

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:05.260
But that was, I think,
only one or two months

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:07.040
after we graduated.

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:09.190
And then all of our
plans were kind of

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:13.510
canceled by COVID closures.

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:17.920
And we actually started
working on simulations

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:22.180
and working with this
digital materiality of things

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:25.270
when we started talking
with Sarah and Heidi

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:28.450
about maybe moving the gallery
to this virtual space, which

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:31.840
ended up being, like, a really
rich and different format that

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:34.510
is inherently a part
of this project.

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:36.130
But it's always
in the background.

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:38.380
It's always kind of hidden.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:41.560
And throughout this--

00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:45.640
I guess thanks to having
this time when we're not

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:47.860
making that many
things, we had the time

00:40:47.860 --> 00:40:50.800
to think about representing
them and turning them

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:54.190
into this different
kind of experience.

00:40:54.190 --> 00:40:54.700
Yeah.

00:40:54.700 --> 00:40:56.800
I mean, also in
terms of the grant,

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:00.040
we were able to make
much more brave choices

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:03.190
in terms of how the
direction of our thesis

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:07.960
was going to go based on
this opportunity that we got.

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:11.570
And had we not had the
grant, for example,

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:14.390
we wouldn't have been brave
enough to say, let's try

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:15.500
and make our own machine.

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:15.830
You know?

00:41:15.830 --> 00:41:16.095
No.

00:41:16.095 --> 00:41:16.595
Yeah.

00:41:16.595 --> 00:41:19.460
And so it was definitely--

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:25.120
gave us the launching
pad for all of our--

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:27.260
Yeah, I think already
as part of our thesis,

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:31.580
we were kind of preparing
for this exhibition.

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:33.950
We always-- we had that in mind.

00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:36.230
And that's why we already
had some of our stands

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:38.990
that Sarah also mentioned
in the beginning.

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:41.240
We were kind of ready to
move into the gallery.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.460
But it's still in
there, I think.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:45.600
[LAUGHS]

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:51.560
And it allowed us to go much
bigger in scale and much braver

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:54.812
in terms of designing
machines or displays.

00:41:54.812 --> 00:41:56.270
And we're really
thankful for that.

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:56.770
Yeah.

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:59.090
Thank you so much.

00:41:59.090 --> 00:42:01.357
I just wanted to say thank
you so much for having us.

00:42:01.357 --> 00:42:03.940
And I think tomorrow we'll also
have some really great events.

00:42:03.940 --> 00:42:06.220
So thank you all for coming out.

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:09.073
And join Arts on the
Radar tomorrow too.

00:42:09.073 --> 00:42:09.820
Yeah.

00:42:09.820 --> 00:42:11.230
Thank you so much, everyone.

00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:13.420
I would love to echo that.

00:42:13.420 --> 00:42:14.630
Thank you all for attending.

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:15.910
Thank you to my co-host Sarah.

00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:17.470
Thank you to Dalma and Jung.

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:19.390
Thank you for
everybody in the Office

